there's a more harmonious tone as allies  meet for the first time since the  pandemic struck  it's a contrast to the discord brought  by donald trump  his successor now rebuilding relations  and rebooting the alliance nato stands  together  that's how we've met every other threat  in the past  it's our greatest strength as we meet  our challenges  of the future and there are many a key  one is russia  with joe biden off to meet vladimir  putin on wednesday  for the first time as president i'm  going to make clear  to president putin that  there are areas where we can't cooperate  if he chooses  and if he chooses not to cooperate  and acts in a way that he has in the  past  relative to cyber security and some  other  activities then we will respond  he's bright he's tough  and uh i have found that uh he is a uh  as they say when he used to play ball a  worthy adversary  seeking to strengthen nato from all  threats allies agreed on plans for the  future  they sounded more ambitious than  anything attempted under the previous  u.s administration  they pledged for the first time to  combat the impact of climate change  and agreed that china presents systemic  challenges to international rules and  values  allies meeting here appear relieved that  donald trump is no longer in the room  but the challenge he posed to nato pales  into insignificance  compared with the one posed not just by  russia but also  by an increasingly assertive china and  by the different ways countries can hurt  each other  from cyber attacks and fake news to  launching  super-fast missiles and even targeting  satellites in space  in fact hostile action in space could  prompt  nato to retaliate just as it would to a  more conventional ground attack  we will make it clear at this summit  that of course any attack  on space capabilities like satellites  and so on  or attacks from space will  could trigger article 5.  that's in a way just to clarify  something which has been  in many ways obvious for 